      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section id="prevention">
        <h2>Prevention: Process Controls and Joint Design Strategies</h2>
        <p>
          Preventing adhesive bond failures at the process level requires control of the
          variables that most strongly influence each failure mode. Surface preparation
          consistency is the single most impactful preventive measure for adhesive failure:
          a documented and validated cleaning and activation protocol, with in-process
          checks such as water break testing or contact angle measurement to confirm surface
          energy before adhesive application, eliminates the majority of interfacial failure
          causes. For cohesive failure prevention, the priority is cure process control —
          adhesive and assembly temperature, mixing ratio verification for two-component
          systems, and bond line thickness within the adhesive's specified range — because
          deviations in any of these parameters can prevent full cohesive strength development
          regardless of the adhesive's formulated performance.
        </p>
        <p>
          At the joint design level, preventing failure requires matching the joint
          geometry to the adhesive's strength characteristics. Adhesives are strong in
          shear and compression, and weaker in peel and cleavage. Joint geometries that
          concentrate stress at the joint edge — such as simple butt joints under tensile
          load — maximise peel and cleavage stress components and are inherently more
          susceptible to adhesive failure initiation at the edge. Lap joints, scarf joints,
          and joggle joints distribute stress more uniformly and use the adhesive's shear
          strength more efficiently. For applications where the joint will see peel-type
          loads in service, specifying a toughened or flexible adhesive grade that can
          absorb peel energy by plastic deformation before fracture significantly improves
          joint durability. As outlined in our overview of
          <a href="/pressure-sensitive-adhesives-acrylics-rubber" target="_top">pressure-sensitive adhesive chemistry</a>,
          even in flexible adhesive systems the balance between peel, tack, and shear
          strength is a fundamental design trade-off that must be addressed at the
          formulation stage.
        </p>

        <h3>Raw Material and Incoming Quality Controls</h3>
        <p>
          Batch-to-batch variation in adhesive chemistry — viscosity, mix ratio tolerance,
          pot life, and surface tack — directly influences which failure mode a joint
          system tends toward in production. Incoming quality checks that verify adhesive
          viscosity, gel time, and a representative lap-shear sample from each batch provide
          early warning of formulation drift before a production run generates failed joints.
          Substrate quality checks should verify surface energy (using dyne test pens or
          contact angle measurement), confirm surface cleanliness meets the cleaning process
          specification, and check substrate thickness and laminate integrity for composite
          materials to avoid substrate failure from pre-existing delamination.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="when-to-escalate">
        <h2>When to Escalate to a Specialist</h2>
        <p>
          Escalation from in-house investigation to specialist failure analysis is
          warranted when the failure mode is inconsistent across a production run — some
          joints showing adhesive failure, others cohesive — because this inconsistency
          points to a process variability source that a single corrective action will not
          resolve. It also applies whenever repeated process corrections to surface
          preparation, cure schedule, and mixing procedures have failed to shift the failure
          mode toward the desired cohesive-dominant pattern.
        </p>
        <p>
          Safety-critical bonded joints — structural aerospace, automotive, medical device,
          and load-bearing construction applications — require forensic-level failure analysis
          whenever unexpected failure modes are observed, including scanning electron
          microscopy of fracture surfaces to map failure mode distribution at micron
          resolution, EDX elemental analysis to identify surface contamination species,
          and differential scanning calorimetry to confirm adhesive cure state. Novel
          adhesive-substrate combinations outside the adhesive manufacturer's documented
          application experience, joints exhibiting different failure modes under accelerated
          ageing conditions versus ambient testing, and failures in service that cannot be
          replicated in laboratory testing all represent cases where specialist material
          and process expertise is necessary to resolve the root cause with confidence.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the difference between adhesive failure and cohesive failure in an adhesive bond?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Adhesive failure occurs at the interface between the adhesive and one of the bonded substrates — the fracture plane runs along the adhesive-substrate boundary, leaving one substrate surface clean and the other coated with adhesive. Cohesive failure occurs through the body of the adhesive itself — the fracture plane runs through the adhesive mass, leaving adhesive residue on both fractured surfaces. The distinction matters for root cause analysis: adhesive failure points to a surface preparation, adhesion promoter, or adhesive-substrate compatibility problem, while cohesive failure points to insufficient adhesive strength, undercure, or in-service degradation of the adhesive after joint formation.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How do I identify adhesive bond failure type from fractured surfaces?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">After separating a bonded joint, examine both fracture surfaces. If one surface is completely clean — substrate material visible, no adhesive residue — and the opposing surface carries the entire adhesive film, the failure is adhesive (interfacial). If both fracture surfaces carry adhesive residue and the fracture plane ran through the adhesive body, the failure is cohesive. If the fracture propagated into the substrate material itself — fibres, laminate plies, or coating layers tearing away — the failure is classified as substrate failure. In practice, mixed-mode failure is common: a peel test might show 70% cohesive and 30% adhesive failure, which still points toward an interfacial weakness but indicates the adhesive strength is adequate at the tested load.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What causes adhesive failure at the substrate interface?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Adhesive failure at the interface typically results from one of four root causes: surface contamination preventing adhesive wetting (oils, release agents, moisture, or oxide layers); insufficient surface energy on the substrate (particularly on low-energy plastics where the adhesive cannot achieve adequate molecular contact without surface treatment); wrong adhesive chemistry for the substrate (mismatched polarity or functional group chemistry preventing interfacial bonding); or inadequate primer or adhesion promoter where one is required by the substrate-adhesive combination. Environmental conditions at the time of bonding — temperature below the adhesive's minimum application temperature, or high relative humidity on moisture-sensitive systems — are also common adhesive failure root causes in production environments.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why does cohesive failure occur even when the adhesive looks well-cured?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Cohesive failure despite apparent cure is most commonly caused by one of three mechanisms: undercure that is not visible on the surface — particularly in thick bond lines or in shadowed areas of UV-cured systems where light penetration is insufficient; additive migration or plasticiser extraction that has softened the adhesive body after cure; or thermal or chemical degradation in service that has reduced the crosslink density. In two-component adhesive systems, off-ratio mixing is a particularly common root cause — an adhesive that appears to have set may have insufficient crosslink density to develop full cohesive strength, a failure that becomes apparent only under mechanical load or after thermal cycling.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is substrate failure in an adhesive test always a sign of a problem?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Not always. In adhesive peel or lap-shear testing, substrate failure — where the bonded material fractures before the adhesive joint — indicates that the adhesive bond strength exceeds the substrate's own strength. This is often the design intent for structural adhesives applied to composite laminates or fibre boards: the joint is stronger than the component, so the component fails first. However, substrate failure becomes a problem when it occurs unexpectedly — indicating the substrate has been weakened by the adhesive or its curing chemistry — or when the joint load case in service is different from the test load case, potentially masking an underlying adhesive or cohesive weakness that would manifest under different stress modes such as peel rather than shear.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How do peel tests and lap-shear tests differ in diagnosing bond failure type?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Peel tests concentrate stress at a moving peel front at the edge of the joint, making them sensitive to interfacial weaknesses and adhesive failure modes — particularly relevant for flexible substrates, tapes, and labels. Lap-shear tests distribute stress across the entire bond area, testing the cohesive strength of the adhesive under shear loading. A joint that passes lap-shear testing but fails peel testing indicates good cohesive strength but susceptibility to edge-initiated adhesive failure — relevant for joints exposed to peel-type stresses in service. Using both test geometries gives a more complete picture of failure mode across different stress states, and is standard practice in adhesive qualification per ISO 4587 and ISO 11339.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>When should an adhesive bond failure investigation be escalated to a specialist?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Escalation is appropriate when the failure mode is mixed or inconsistent across a production batch — some joints failing adhesively and others cohesively — which points to a process variability issue requiring systematic investigation. Safety-critical applications where bond failure carries structural or life-safety consequences should always involve specialist failure analysis, including scanning electron microscopy of fracture surfaces, EDX analysis for contamination identification, and FTIR examination of adhesive chemistry. Escalation is also warranted where repeated process corrections have failed to resolve the failure mode, where failure is occurring in service rather than in acceptance testing, and where the adhesive-substrate combination is outside the adhesive manufacturer's tested application range.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

